  "query_preview": "../Saturday through Monday/...At least the first half \nof the long term forecast remains largely uneventful as an area of\nhigh pressure persists over the Gulf through Saturday, bringing \nnorthwesterly flow, drier conditions, and warmer temperatures. The\nentire panhandle will see mainly clear skies Friday into midday \nSaturday, with cloud cover moving in from the north by Saturday \nevening. After some stronger winds into Thursday night, especially\nover the northern inner channels, winds will decrease by Friday \nmorning before the flow switches southerly through the afternoon. \nFor the weekend, southerly winds in Lynn Canal will increase to \naround 20 kts with the help of sea breeze formation from midday \ninto the early evening hours. High temperatures Friday will reach \nthe upper 60s to low 70s in the northern panhandle and potentially\nwell into the 70s in the southern panhandle under ample clearing.\nSome areas still look like they may reach the upper 70s Friday \nand Saturday, mainly in the vicinity of Hyder. \n\nModels have come into somewhat better agreement in regarding to the \ntiming of the next frontal trough. The Canadian still wants to break \ndown the ridge soonest, but even it has slowed. We have higher \nconfidence that conditions will remain mainly dry for the majority \nof the area through Sunday, except perhaps along the northeast Gulf \ncoast. Rain chances will then gradually increase from northwest to \nsoutheast across the region on Monday as the pattern turns more \nactive.",
